 # Voxel Interpolation (Nearest, Trilinear, Triquadratic, Tricubic)
+
+
 ## Overview
-Voxel interpolation samples values from a dense or sparse 3D volume grid at arbitrary floating-point coordinates using polynomial interpolation of varying orders (nearest, trilinear, triquadratic, tricubic) [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of extracting continuous scalar or vector fields from discrete voxel representations, which is essential for volume rendering and signed distance field (SDF) evaluation [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its parent [Voxel Trilinear Interpolation (SIMD)](2564-voxel-trilinear-interpolation-simd.md) by supporting multiple interpolation orders (0–3) and integrating with high-level tensor frameworks like PyTorch or low-level tree structures like OpenVDB/NanoVDB [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S3].
+Voxel interpolation samples values from a dense or sparse 3D volume grid at arbitrary floating-point coordinates using polynomial interpolation of varying orders (nearest, trilinear, triquadratic, tricubic) [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of extracting continuous scalar or vector fields from discrete voxel representations, which is essential for volume rendering, signed distance field evaluation, and spatial transformer networks [S1, S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its parent [Voxel Trilinear Interpolation (SIMD)](2564-voxel-trilinear-interpolation-simd.md) by supporting multiple interpolation orders (0–3) and integrating with high-level tensor frameworks like PyTorch or low-level tree structures like OpenVDB/NanoVDB, rather than being limited to a single SIMD-optimized trilinear kernel [S1, S2, unofficial].
+
 ## How it works
+The method operates in two primary contexts: high-level tensor sampling (PyTorch) and low-level tree-based sampling (OpenVDB/NanoVDB) [S1, S2, unofficial][S3]. Both require coordinate normalization and boundary handling [G].
